  - [1mTime-based reminders[0m — due dates, overdue tasks, "this is due in an hour." No second actor needed; works fine
    offline in one browser. This is the only reading that's fully coherent with what exists today, and it's the smallest
    build. It's also not really a "notifications system" in the change-notification sense — it's a scheduler.
  - [1mMulti-user collaboration[0m — someone else edits a shared task. This is the literal reading of your request, but it
    requires a server, accounts, and sync. That's a much larger project, and it makes the client-side-only answer above
    no longer true.
  - [1mSame user, multiple tabs or devices[0m — you edit in one tab, another tab is stale. Cross-tab is cheap
    (BroadcastChannel, no server). Cross-device needs a server, same as collaboration.
  - [1mAutomation changes[0m — recurring tasks rolling over, rules firing. Needs a rules engine before it needs notifications.

  The expensive-to-reverse part: reminders and cross-tab stay client-only forever, while collaboration and cross-device
  mean a server, auth, and a sync model — which reshapes the task storage design underneath, not just the notification
  layer. Picking wrong here is a rewrite, not a refactor.

  My recommendation is [1mtime-based reminders[0m, on the grounds that it's the only one that delivers something real against
  a static page with no backend, and it doesn't foreclose the others. But I'd rather hear that I've misread your intent
  — if you actually mean collaboration, say so and we'll scope the server properly instead of pretending it's small.
